\section{Mechanism Analysis}
\label{app:mechanism_discussion}

Three non-exclusive mechanisms may drive the observed 13.5--29.2\% temporal DIF rate (after non-independence corrections): (1)~differential contamination, where newer models encounter benchmark items during training; (2)~capability evolution, where training advances shift item--ability relationships; (3)~training methodology shifts (e.g., RLHF, instruction tuning). The ETS baseline of 5--15\% C\%~\citep{clauser1998dif} applies to concurrent demographic groups in educational testing; comparison to temporal LLM cohorts is illustrative, not normative. Observational DIF alone cannot separate these mechanisms~\citep{suk2026rethinking}; the evidence below constrains the space of explanations.

\paragraph{Exclusion arguments.}
Four exclusion arguments narrow possible explanations. \emph{Ability confounds}: at most 2.4~pp attributable to ability mismatch (matched C\% $= 28.9\%$ vs.\ unmatched 31.3\%; Cohen's $d = -0.17$; \Cref{sec:temporal}). \emph{Domain-level improvement}: domain accuracy gains explain ${<}0.1\%$ of item-level DIF variance (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$); a Simpson's paradox confirms DIF is item-specific (OR reverses from 1.55 to 0.73 after difficulty control). \emph{Item quality defects}: no enrichment among expert-annotated errors~\citep{gema2024mmlu} (enrichment $= 1.10$, 95\% CI $[0.92, 1.27]$, $p = 0.23$). \emph{Web-overlap labels}: near-independent of DIF flags (Jaccard $= 0.206$, enrichment $= 0.964$; \Cref{sec:groundtruth}).

\paragraph{Difficulty--direction interaction.}
The aggregate difficulty--direction correlation ($\rho = -0.597$; \Cref{sec:temporal}) shows that hardest-decile DIF-C items are 88.7\% focal-favoring while easiest-decile items are 96.2\% reference-favoring. This gradient is robust under range restriction ($\rho = -0.447$ at difficulty $\in [0.2, 0.8]$; $p < 10^{-100}$). Semi-synthetic analysis (\Cref{sec:semisynthetic}) reveals that uniform injection produces $\rho \approx -0.52$ via ceiling effects (high-accuracy items have fewer flippable responses), while difficulty-dependent injection yields $\rho \approx +0.77$ (zero overlap across seeds). The observed $\rho$ thus cannot alone distinguish contamination from capability evolution.

\paragraph{Difficulty--model-type interaction.}
\label{app:interaction}
Logistic regression confirms that the base-vs-instruct direction reversal is not a difficulty confound: the difficulty $\times$ model-type interaction is significant ($\hat{\beta} = 1.56$, OR $= 4.75$, $p < 10^{-188}$; $N = 8{,}585$ items). Controlling for difficulty, model type remains a strong independent predictor of DIF direction (OR $= 4.24$, $p < 10^{-225}$). Among 2,053 items flagged DIF-C in both base and instruct analyses, 98\% show concordant direction; the aggregate reversal arises from \emph{which} items reach C threshold, not from individual items reversing direction.

\paragraph{Mixed-mechanism injection.}
\label{app:mixed_mechanism}
To test the hypothesis that different contamination mechanisms operate on base and instruct models simultaneously, we designed mixed injection schemes: Scheme~D applies difficulty-dependent injection to base models and uniform injection to instruct models. Across 20 seeds, Scheme~D reproduces the overall difficulty--direction gradient ($\rho = -0.676 \pm 0.005$ vs.\ observed $-0.597$) and direction reversal ($\chi^2 = 634 \pm 184$ vs.\ observed $562.7$), but cannot match the near-zero instruct gradient: simulated $\rho_{\mathrm{instruct}} = -0.302 \pm 0.090$ vs.\ observed $-0.079$. This residual gap ($3.8\times$ the observed value) persists across all tested injection parameters, suggesting that instruct-model DIF is at least partially driven by non-contamination mechanisms (e.g., alignment-induced capability shifts) rather than memorization alone.

\section{Structural Analysis Details}
\label{app:structural}

Two structural controls and a sensitivity check fail to reduce the temporal C\% below 31\%, revealing a structural property of the data rather than a methodological limitation. Matched-ability quintile analysis restricts comparisons to models of similar total score and yields enrichment ratios of 0.94--1.04, with temporal C\% unchanged at approximately 0.31. Within-subject DIF analysis on 5 representative subjects, intended to isolate subject-specific effects, finds mean C\% $= 38.1\%$, exceeding the 25\% threshold that would indicate viable bifactor structure. As a sensitivity check, IRT $\theta$-conditioning with 20 ability strata achieves $r(\theta, \mathrm{total\ score}) = 0.9986$ and C\% $= 0.312$, confirming that the external total score already captures nearly all ability variance relevant to DIF computation. \Cref{tab:structural_comparison} summarizes these results.

The temporal FPR reflects genuine item-level instability that persists regardless of how ability is measured or conditioned. Yen's Q3 diagnostic reveals mild local dependence within subjects (11.3\% of within-subject item pairs exceed $|Q3| > 0.20$, vs.\ 2.2\% cross-subject; $5{\times}$ ratio, $p = 2.3 \times 10^{-9}$), which contributes to elevated within-subject DIF rates but is not the primary driver: the cross-subject matching protocol addresses this bias, and temporal C\% persists under external matching (full Q3 distributions available in the supplementary material).

